### Description

Two of the `ui` color overrides a custom theme can set — `ui.focus` and `ui.active` — are rejected by settings validation, even though the `CustomTheme` interface declares them and the theme resolver already reads both. A custom theme that uses either key fails to start.

This is the same defect as #25689, which was fixed for `text.response` in #25822. That fix covered one key of the `ui`/`text` drift; these two are still live.

### Steps to reproduce

1. Add a custom theme to `settings.json`:

```json
{
  "ui": {
    "theme": "FocusTest",
    "customThemes": {
      "FocusTest": {
        "type": "custom",
        "name": "FocusTest",
        "ui": {
          "focus": "#00FFFF",
          "active": "#0000FF"
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

2. Start the CLI.

Actual: validation fails with

```
Invalid configuration: Unrecognized key(s) in object: 'focus'
```

Expected: the theme loads, `focus` sets the focus color and `active` the active color.

### Root cause

`packages/core/src/config/config.ts:329-335` declares all five `ui` keys on the `CustomTheme` interface:

```ts
  ui?: {
    comment?: string;
    symbol?: string;
    active?: string;
    focus?: string;
    gradient?: string[];
  };
```

and the theme resolver reads both of the missing ones — `packages/cli/src/ui/themes/theme.ts:434` (`FocusColor: customTheme.ui?.focus ?? customTheme.AccentGreen`) and `:603` (`active: customTheme.ui?.active ?? colors.AccentBlue`).

But the validation schema's `ui` object in `packages/cli/src/config/settingsSchema.ts` lists only `comment`, `symbol` and `gradient`, with `additionalProperties: false`, so both keys are rejected. The committed hosted schema `schemas/settings.schema.json` has the same gap, so editor completion and any external validation see it too.

Note this is a TypeScript-valid configuration that the runtime refuses, which is why it is a bug rather than a missing feature: the interface and the schema have drifted.

### Related observation

`ripGrep.ts` and `grep.ts` have byte-identical error handling for a failed search. One of them marks the failure; the other does not.

```ts
// packages/core/src/tools/grep.ts:323-334
    } catch (error) {
      debugLogger.warn(`Error during GrepLogic execution: ${error}`);
      const errorMessage = getErrorMessage(error);
      return {
        llmContent: `Error during grep search operation: ${errorMessage}`,
        returnDisplay: `Error: ${errorMessage}`,
        error: {
          message: errorMessage,
          type: ToolErrorType.GREP_EXECUTION_ERROR,
        },
      };
    }
```

```ts
// packages/core/src/tools/ripGrep.ts:348-355 — same block, no `error`
    } catch (error) {
      debugLogger.warn(`Error during GrepLogic execution: ${error}`);
      const errorMessage = getErrorMessage(error);
      return {
        llmContent: `Error during grep search operation: ${errorMessage}`,
        returnDisplay: `Error: ${errorMessage}`,
      };
    }
```

**Why that matters.** `ToolResult.error` is documented as "If this property is present, the tool call is considered a failure" (`tools.ts:770-772`), and that is exactly how it is consumed — `tool-executor.ts:150` branches on `toolResult.error === undefined` into `createSuccessResult` (`status: CoreToolCallStatus.Success`, no error type) versus `createErrorResult`.

`RipGrepTool` is the one registered by default whenever ripgrep is available (`config.ts:4028`); `GrepTool` is only the fallback, logged as `RipgrepFallbackEvent`. So the unmarked path is the common one.

The most reachable way into it is a search timeout: `ripGrep.ts:294` throws `Operation timed out after <n>ms` from inside the same outer `try`, and that lands in the catch above. The model is told the search failed, and the harness records the call as a success with no error type — so there is no error badge, and any accounting keyed on tool-call status counts it as a pass.

