### What happened?

With telemetry written to a file (`GEMINI_TELEMETRY_TARGET=local` plus `GEMINI_TELEMETRY_OUTFILE`), most metric data points in the file have `"endTime": "[Circular]"` instead of a timestamp, and histogram bucket `boundaries` are written as `"[Circular]"` too.

The cause is `safeJsonStringify` in `packages/core/src/utils/safeJsonStringify.ts`. It adds every object it visits to a `WeakSet` and never removes it, so any object that is referenced a second time is replaced with `"[Circular]"`, even when there is no cycle:

```ts
const shared = { x: 1 };
safeJsonStringify({ a: shared, b: shared });
// {"a":{"x":1},"b":"[Circular]"}
```

The OpenTelemetry SDK reuses one `endTime` array for all data points in a collection, and one `boundaries` array per histogram, so every data point after the first loses them. The file exporters started using `safeJsonStringify` in #19244 to get past the real span processor cycle from #19228.

Other callers have the same problem whenever an object is shared, for example `RecordingContentGenerator` (`--record-responses`) and the `gen_ai` tool definition attributes in `LoggingContentGenerator`.

Repro on main (09e048fd6), headless, no account needed:

```bash
cat > fake.responses <<'EOF'
{"method":"generateContentStream","response":[{"candidates":[{"content":{"parts":[{"text":"Hello there."}],"role":"model"},"finishReason":"STOP","index":0}],"usageMetadata":{"promptTokenCount":10,"candidatesTokenCount":3,"totalTokenCount":13}}]}
{"method":"generateContent","response":{"candidates":[{"content":{"parts":[{"text":"{}"}],"role":"model"},"finishReason":"STOP","index":0}]}}
{"method":"countTokens","response":{"totalTokens":10}}
EOF
GEMINI_CLI_TRUST_WORKSPACE=true GEMINI_TELEMETRY_ENABLED=true GEMINI_TELEMETRY_TARGET=local \
  GEMINI_TELEMETRY_OUTFILE=$PWD/telemetry.log GEMINI_API_KEY=fake \
  node packages/cli/dist/index.js -p "say hello" --fake-responses-non-strict $PWD/fake.responses
